---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ExternalLink from "../components/ExternalLink.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Soundscapes of Alaska — an unofficial showcase of NPS Alaska acoustic monitoring highlights.">
  <h1>About Soundscapes of Alaska</h1>

  <h2>About This Site</h2>
  <p>
    This site was created to showcase soundscapes from across
    National Parks in the <ExternalLink href="https://www.nps.gov/state/ak/index.htm">Alaska Region</ExternalLink>.
    Raw audio was collected as part of National Park Service <ExternalLink href="https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1050/index.htm">Natural Sounds and Night Skies Division</ExternalLink>
    (NSNSD) Type 1 acoustic monitoring data. <ExternalLink href="https://www.nps.gov/subjects/sound/measure.htm">How does NSNSD measure sounds?</ExternalLink>. 
    
    Specific clips were then manually selected and edited. Special thanks to Halyn Betchkal for picking out these (and many more) audio highlights over the years.

    "If a tree falls in the woods, and no one is around to hear it... it may have been recorded!"
    <br />
    For example, at <a href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL + 'clips/denamoos_20180814_105458/'}>this Denali monitoring site!</a>.

  </p>

  <h2>How to Read a Spectrogram</h2>
  <div class="placeholder-box">
    <!-- TODO: write spectrogram guide -->
    <ul>
      <li><ExternalLink href="https://www.learngala.com/cases/echoes-of-elevation/6">Introduction to reading spectrograms!</ExternalLink></li>

      <li>Time on the x-axis, frequency aka pitch on the y-axis, brightness = loudness at that time/frequency band</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>How does the National Park Service find these sounds?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>As detailed above, the National Park Service collects acoustic data for monitoring and resource protection. As part
    of these efforts, data is annotated to highlight the time periods when human noise events occur. This annotation happens visually via spectrograms of the sound data. 
    Data annotators do NOT listen to every single minute of recorded audio as that would take many, MANY hours.</li>
    <li>Along the way - the person annotating discovers lots of other sounds, and they may save separate audio clips 
    of the most interesting ones to share with the world!</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  h1 {
    color: var(--color-forest);
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  h2 {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    color: var(--color-forest);
  }

  .placeholder-box ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
  }
</style>
